 "_guide_frame": "6656 x 4096 px = the whole 5 x 5 grid at the play camera (orthographic, pitch 52.95354112560294, yaw 47): 100.6176 px/m across, 80.3076 per ground metre up-screen",
 "_pins": "fid/pt/ph3/pins_ph3.json (guide + ID + class COPIES + 25 tiles, LV 67fc4b80b, R-C9-284: natural floes for the honeycomb; tiles changed vs b5894d440: ['0_3', '1_3', '2_3', '0_4', '1_4', '2_4', '3_4']; the 9 pilot tiles unchanged, R-C9-189). Previous: pins_ph3_b5894d440.json",
 "_rules_source": "v1 rules + fid/v1tools/DEV12_substitutions.json (R-C9-258: substitution 1 only; palette = v1's five); cfg_check.py enforces",
 "_refs_source": "v1 refs verbatim (IMAGE 2 = the approved Frost King's Barrow concept); no DEV-3/11 plates",
 "geo": "IMAGE 1 is not a flat-colour layout: it is a GREYBOX RENDER of a real 3D game level, seen from the game's fixed high three-quarter ORTHOGRAPHIC camera (53 degrees down), at true scale -- the small painted barbarian among the standing stones is 1.85 m tall. Every grey or coloured form is a real object that will be modelled at exactly this position and size. LIGHT WARM GREY ground = deep, smooth snow, warm peach-cream where lit, with distinct lavender / blue-violet shadow shapes and rounded cobble cells; LIGHT BLUE-GREY = the frozen mere, mid-blue lapis ice, mostly unbroken, with a few soft pale veins, low snowy islands in it; WHITE-BLUE ground = shore-fast ice breaking into plates and floes, DARK SLATE-BLUE = open water between them, and the narrow dark sliver at the cliff foot beside the shingle flank is a natural irregular lead; MUTED OLIVE-BROWN PATCHES = reddish-brown winter heather in clumps of rounded tufts with snow between; STRAW patches = tall straw-gold reed beds; the LIGHT TAN SLOPE = a grey shingle beach under rounded snow drifts; SMALL GREY UPRIGHT SLABS = six carved standing stones (spiral and knotwork carvings on the faces turned toward the viewer), two fallen; the LARGE DOME = the barrow mound, earth and dead grass under thin snow, its carved stone door in front; the BROWN HULL = an old longship wreck heeled over in the shore ice; the GREY ROCK MASSES on the coast = weathered granite sea cliffs, snow on every ledge, a dark sea cave and rough steps in a cleft; the BROWN TIMBER building = a half-burned longhall, a charred gable and trampled ash beside it; thin brown posts = a broken palisade. The ONLY dark openings in the whole scene are the barrow's great door, the longhall's great door and the sea cave: paint no other doorway, gap or opening anywhere.",
 "rules": "Paint OVER the render: every object keeps EXACTLY its silhouette, position and size (the 3D models are placed from this same layout, so anything moved will not match); shadows stay where the render puts them (one winter sun from the upper LEFT, 55 degrees up, short soft blue-violet shadows touching their objects). Paint the barbarian OUT -- he is there only for scale; leave open snow where he stands. Keep the stone ring's centre, the barrow door's approach and the mere OPEN: no new rocks, trees or drifts on them. Small scatter is welcome only inside the scrub patches and at the foot of rocks and stones. One thin warm dark-brown ink line on every contour, the SAME weight everywhere. Palette and hand of IMAGE 2 (the approved Frost King's Barrow concept): cold blue-white snow, warm grey granite, rust heather, dark juniper, lapis ice; watercolour washes with pen detail. No sky, no horizon, no text, UI, border or characters.",
